<h2>The setting</h2>
<p>The applicant sought anticipatory bail (Section 438 CrPC, now Section 482 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023) in a cheating FIR of 2023. Two features framed the hearing before Justice Girish Kathpalia. First, on 20 May 2026 the same Court had granted anticipatory bail to the co-accused — the applicant's wife — observing that the case "appears to be only a case of civil liability", while expressly leaving the trial court free to take an independent view after the chargesheet. Second, between the previous hearing and the decision, the applicant had joined the investigation pursuant to interim directions.</p>

<h2>What decided the application</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Interim protection, then cooperation.</strong> The applicant appeared before the Investigating Officer and participated in the investigation rather than litigating from a distance.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. The IO's stand changed.</strong> On the decisive date, the Investigating Officer informed the Court that because of the cooperation, arrest was no longer required.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. The prosecution did not oppose.</strong> With custodial interrogation off the table and a co-accused already protected, the State had no surviving ground of opposition.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Bail allowed on light terms.</strong> Personal bond of Rs. 10,000 with one surety in the like amount, to the satisfaction of the IO/SHO.</div>
</div>

<h2>The parity dimension</h2>
<p>The earlier protection granted to the co-accused wife mattered in two ways. As a matter of parity, an applicant in a similar position to a protected co-accused ordinarily cannot be treated more harshly without a distinguishing role. And as a matter of substance, the observation that the dispute appeared civil in character — made while protecting the co-accused — travelled naturally into the applicant's case, since the allegations arose from the same transaction. Delhi courts routinely weigh both strands, while being careful to record that such observations do not bind the trial court.</p>

<h2>Why conduct between hearings matters</h2>
<p>The modern anticipatory bail framework is built on the distinction between arrest for investigation and arrest as routine. Where an accused demonstrates, in real time, that the investigation can proceed fully with his participation but without his custody, the foundation for opposing pre-arrest bail erodes. The pattern visible in this judgment is one practitioners see constantly in the Delhi High Court:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Appear, don't abscond</h3><p>Interim protection is an opportunity to demonstrate reliability. Non-appearance or evasive answers convert a winnable application into a rejected one.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Documents over arguments</h3><p>In commercial-flavour FIRs, producing the ledger, agreement or payment trail during investigation often does more than any precedent cited at the bar.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The IO's status report</h3><p>The single most influential document at the second hearing is the status report recording whether the applicant joined and cooperated.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Conditions are promises</h3><p>Bail granted on cooperation assumes continued cooperation; a later breach invites cancellation under the settled framework for setting aside or cancelling bail.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The civil-liability observation, kept in its place</h2>
<p>Courts are careful with the line between a commercial dispute and cheating: the offence under Section 420 IPC (now Section 318(4) BNS) requires dishonest inducement at the inception of the transaction, not a mere failure to perform a promise. At the anticipatory bail stage, a court does not decide that question; it asks only whether the allegations, as they stand, justify custodial interrogation. Where the record suggests accounts and agreements rather than deception from the outset, that tentative assessment supports pre-arrest protection — as it did here — while the final characterisation is left to the trial court on the chargesheet.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Practice note:</strong> an anticipatory bail applicant in Delhi should treat the first interim order as the beginning of the case, not the end. The period between hearings is when the application is actually decided — by attendance before the IO, by the quality of answers and documents, and by the status report those produce. This article is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
